Usage 'get'

We can use 'get + a person + to infinitive' to mean 'persuade someone to do something or make someone do something'.

 I'll get my dad to take me in the car. (I'll persuade my dad to take me in the car.)

He got the children to tidy their room. (We made the children tidy their room.)

Can you get Julie to come to the party? (Can you persuade Julie to come to the party?)

We got the teacher to explain again. (We persuaded the teacher to explain again.)

​

Let's review!

​
Rewrite these sentences so they use 'get' to mean 'persuade or make'. Use short forms where possible.

​

1. I didn't make the children tidy up yesterday.

___________________________________________________________________________

2. Did he persuade you to cook dinner?

___________________________________________________________________________

3. You persuaded me to do it last time.

________________________________________________________________________
​

Answers

​

1. I didn't get the children to tidy up yesterday.

2. Did he get you to cook dinner?

3. You got me to do it last time.

(Students must avoid bare infinitives in the sentence - types given above. )

